verifier.best_fix:
Keep the auditor's watch but make its persistence about T_REL/2, and gate the connect on it:

```verilog
if (!o_net || !claimed) lost <= 0;
else if (sp_fresh && sp_port == trunk) lost <= sp_m ? 0 : sat(lost+1);
...
else if (claimed && lost_full) begin o_after <= O_NOGO; nst <= N_DROP; end   // in N_NET/N_WAITCL, after o_gone
if (claimed && o_last && dmo == 0 && lost == 0) nst <= N_DONE;
```

On the board, 'full' is 128 passes, which is 128 ms. In the bench, scaled by 8, it is 16 passes (rtl_alt/net_unit.v uses a 5-bit counter at bit 4). That is longer than any dial break (at most about 70 ms), so glare stays 'joined' as documented. It is also longer than any plausible gap between a port's RC T_REL and the register's counted CLR, so r_gone always wins for a caller who hangs up. It is shorter than T_REL, so the NOGO lands before the dead trunk can clear itself and be hunted. The gated N_DONE never connects a caller to a trunk whose claim is low at the last digit, so the residual window is only a loss within T_ARM after the N_DONE write. Cost: one 8-bit counter (a '393, or a tap on an existing ms counter), the existing claim comparator, and one AND term, which is the same package count as the auditor's 3-bit version. Tested at scale: S4 is closed, S7 gives 12/12 at T_REL 32 and at T_REL 26, tb_net is 88/88 and tb_lfull 40/40. Two trade-offs for Joel. First, a shorter persistence (roughly 40 ms, still above the RC skew) would also abandon calls hit by the 8-256 ms link glitches of C07 after the claim, but it turns glare into cannot-complete, which is his call. Second, C17's fix (CLR a few ms shorter than T_REL) is complementary, and it is what the auditor's 4-sample version would need in order to be safe.

findings.notes:
This is one root cause with three symptoms, all from the same stale resolver:
- the stale R_GONE at the mend (var0);
- the stale R_LEG during the outage, which drops other old legs and writes the way back onto the dead incoming (var1);
- the old legs staying `mine` (a one-pass delay to classification only).

The phantom seizure at the W neighbour after a var1 mend lasts for the unrelated call's duration: monitors and resolvers have no timeout for a steady M. That the W side would then flood it network-wide is inferred from the code; I did not simulate it in a multi-exchange bench.

findings.evidence:
The cited lines all match the snapshot. net_unit.v:616-617 forwards only if fst==F_IDLE && rs_free_any at the C_ACT write, and nothing retries later. :737-741 take a resolver for a one-leg forward too (rs_two). Resolvers are freed only by R_LEG, R_GONE (:681-708) or rs_mine (:659-665).

My sc4 re-run matches the auditor's log exactly, apart from walltime:
- n_nofwd 0->1 at 111.295 ms (W12) and 1->2 at 152.123 ms (N77);
- both are held with src 00 bsy 1, and mon_held stays 11, so neither is watched;
- the freed resolver is reused by N76;
- everything is released afterwards, 10/10 PASS.

My sc5 re-run is identical too. In 5d the origin sweep runs 7688.0-7700.0, flood 41 is classified at 7689.6, and its forward sweep runs 7700-7712. Flood 30 is classified at 7708.495 while fst=F_HUNT, with both resolvers free: n_nofwd +1, and the 5d check FAILs.

With NRES=3, the third flood W12 is forwarded (legs 76/42/32) and only N77 is lost (n_nofwd +1). The 2 FAILs are that bench's NRES=2-specific checks.

The doc contradiction is out of date. The new exchange/network.md §3.5 says a resolver is taken for every forward, one leg or three, and 'held and watched but never forwarded'. §10 lists 'Resolver sizing, and silent loss at a transit' as Open. The RTL header (net_unit.v:69-71) also documents the one forwarder.

