findings.real_timing:
Real timers. The hub load study measured a resolver hold of 4486 ms for a doomed flood against 1838 ms for a claimed one (2.44x, and 2.4x at scale 8).

The claimed positive feedback on links and resolvers does not show up in this RTL. Every overload run (work/load/overload, transit T6/T7, hub LB/LC) has the one-register exchanges (C15) saturating first:
- links never fill;
- chain transits show nofwd_busy 0 and nomon 0;
- goodput collapses from far-register refusals.

Only at the hub in the LC overload run (about 10x design) did resolvers run out (NORES 26%).

Real timers, and a documented limit:
- monitor.md 4: 'none free: the seizure goes unwatched here, for good';
- new network.md §3.4: 'with no cell free the seizure is still held and forwarded, but this exchange cannot own the call';
- new network.md §10: the pool must be sized again.

A cell is held about 1.5-1.7 s for most floods, since it is released at e's ID. Monitor load is therefore lower than the resolvers': 8 exchanges 0.14 E, NMON=2 0.9% (Engset 0.6%); 20 exchanges 0.39 E, 5.1% (4.6%), with NMON=3 0.66%. The auditor's 0.26 E / 2.6% is a point in their Erlang B table, not the load of an 8-exchange network. The hub study's s1_burst at real timers showed the 3rd flood mon=0.

skeptics.fix_assessment:
A document fix with no RTL change is right. A flood with no identity cannot be made loop-safe in band, and the verifier's three rejected alternatives were correctly rejected.

Changes I would make:

(1) The commissioning check's pass criterion as written is wrong. 'Exactly one claim at the owner, no REFUSED after TAKEN' fails the documented both-ends design for every call between the two strapped ends. The verifier's own control shows it: jobC_both.out has 'claims Y2' and owner_refused=07 in 12/12, and hub G2 shows the same for B<->C. The hub control pairs never included B<->C. Calls between the two ends claim twice with one end strapped or both, so they cannot tell the two apart. The test should instead:
- make calls between each strapped end and a third exchange, in both directions;
- pass when the owner claims once and the origin classifies no seizure of its own flood. On an idle network this shows on the lamp card as an extra incoming trunk going BUSY at the origin.
The anomaly appears only for calls from or to the end that has the jumper, so both ends must be tested.

(2) The jumper is on the net_unit card, beside the e b code jumpers. Repeat the ring test after any unit card is replaced.

(3) network.md §7 should give the real cost of one end:
- double delivery both ways;
- a double claim on every such call;
- intermittent, bursty cannot-complete;
- doubled cell occupancy that starves other calls;
- never a wrong number.
It should also cite these runs in place of 'Built, and not run'. Keep 'must form a tree' as a hard rule.

Optional, cheap in 74HC: an event word when two claims land within a few ms and neither copy came in on a strapped direction. It needs one bit per cell, set from cls_dir at classification, plus a small ms counter. In a correctly strapped network every double has one copy on a strapped port, so this catches an unstrapped ring (F1), and X's calls under a one-end strap (F2), in service.

The only way to make it one physical strap per link would be to carry the strap on the link and read it into the trunk word. That contradicts §7's choice and needs a conductor on every carrier, so it is not worth it.
